<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Analytic Capacity and Rational Approximation</title>

  <p>
    The material in this section is not directly related to the project, but illustrates another motivation for studying analytic capacity
  </p>

  <p>
    It can be difficult to perform certain calculations, such as computing the derivative, with analytic functions. For this reason we seek to approximate a given analytic function by a sequence of simpler functions, which are much easier to work with, so that we can replace the calculation with the analytic function with the calculation with a simpler function and obtain an answer which is almost the same as the exact answer. As it turns out, rational functions work well for this purpose.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-runge">
    <title>Runge's Theorem</title>
    
    
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>K</m> be a compact subset of <m>\mathbb C</m> and suppose <m>f</m> is analytic on <m>K</m>. If <m>A</m> is a set containing at least one complex number from each connected component of <m>\hat{\mathbb C} \setminus K</m> then there exists a sequence <m>\{f_n\}</m> of rational functions that converges uniformly to <m>f</m> in <m>K</m> and whose poles are points in <m>A</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <definition xml:id="def-uniform-convergence">
    <title>Uniform Convergence</title>
    
    
    <statement>
        <p>
            A sequence <m>\{f_n\}</m> is said to <term>converge uniformly</term> to a function <m>f</m> on <m>K</m> if for every <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there exists <m>N \in \mathbb N</m> such that whenever <m>n &gt; N</m>,
            <md>
                |f_n(z)-f(z)| &lt; \epsilon
            </md>
            for all <m>z \in K</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
<p>
  To better understand approximation by rational functions, we consider the following spaces of functions
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-R(x)">
  <title>R(X)</title>
  
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>X</m> be a compact subset of <m>\mathbb C</m> and let <m>R_0(X)</m> denote the set of rational functions with poles outside <m>X</m>. The function space <m>R(X)</m> is the uniform closure of <m>R_0(X)</m>. This means that <m>R(X)</m> consists of all functions that can be uniformly approximated by rational functions with poles off <m>X</m>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

  </section>